## Verdict

Pass. No blocking security finding.
My earlier nit B-D1-1 stays open and goes to a later Green round.

## What changed

The code change is test code only.
`git diff --name-only caac6888 7030ba6a` lists 15 paths, all under measure/.
No product, test, package or lock file changed.

Commit 56e84d8a changes audit-gate.mjs in two places.
`coveringEntry` at line 196 now requires the pkg-keyed entry to hold the advisory id as well as the package.
Line 191 already required both fields for the id-keyed entry.
Both key types now bind the same two fields.
Any other case returns null, so `decide` at lines 211-238 pushes a FAIL.

The self-test grows from 13 to 14 checks.
Line 324 proves that a pkg-keyed entry with another advisory id does not cover the advisory.
Lines 328-332 use future date 2027-02-30 and assert the calendar error text.
That test now proves the calendar rule fires instead of the past-date rule.

## Security effect of the change

Input handling is unchanged.
Line 36 and lines 377-378 still spawn npm with an array argument list and no shell.
Line 426 still builds the snapshot path from the fixed folder, a validated key and an ISO stamp.
Those line numbers moved only because the self-test grew.

Fail-closed behavior is stronger, not weaker.
`isRecord` at lines 96-101 rejects null, functions and arrays.
The pkg lookup key always starts with "pkg:", so it can never be a prototype key.
A network or JSON failure still exits non-zero.

The pkg-key residual from my last review is closed.
A pkg-keyed entry now covers one advisory only, when id and package both match.
The three sharp entries pass the stricter rule with unchanged data.
The date 2026-12-15 is a real calendar date.

